  "query_preview": "..\nCoverage of storms remain isolated at this hour, but could\nbecome more scattered in nature along the International Border.\nMain threats with storms today is lightning, brief heavy rain,\nand gusty winds to 35 mph or so. Any activity should stay\nconfined to the Santa Catalina/Rincon Mtns and south of Tucson. \n\nThe rest of this weekend into early next week continue to look\nlimited, with the best chances south and east of Tucson, where \nthe best instability is. With the above normal moisture, locally\nheavy rainfall leading to flash flooding continue to be the \nprimary impacts with these storms.\n\nAs we move into next week, especially by the mid to late week,\nmoisture/instability builds a bit more. Flow turns more from \nthe SE/ESE, which could bring an upswing in daily showers/storms.\nThe tropics remain active in the EastPac, with another tropical\nsystem becoming likely south of Baja and off the central \nMexican coast. We will keep an eye on this as we move into next \nweek. \n\nTemperatures will be within a couple of degrees of normal through \nWednesday, then dropping to below normal levels late next week.",
